+### Generating Kubernetes manifests from Python format strings
+
+You can also generate Kubernetes sources from a Python format string template. Activate the
+experimental backend in `pants.toml`:
+
+```toml title="pants.toml"
+[GLOBAL]
+backend_packages = [
+  ...
+  "pants.backend.experimental.codegen.python_format_string",
+  "pants.backend.experimental.codegen.python_format_string.k8s",
+]
+```
+
+This adds the [`python_format_string`](../../reference/targets/python_format_string.mdx) and
+[`python_format_strings`](../../reference/targets/python_format_strings.mdx) target types. A
+`python_format_string` target takes a single source file and a required `values` mapping of
+strings, and renders the file using Python's `str.format` substitution:
+
+```yaml title="src/k8s/deployment.yaml"
+kind: Deployment
+metadata:
+  name: my-app
+spec:
+  template:
+    spec:
+      containers:
+        - name: my-app
+          image: my-image:{VERSION}
+```
+
+```python title="src/k8s/BUILD"
+python_format_string(
+    name="deployment",
+    source="deployment.yaml",
+    values={"VERSION": "1.2.3"},
+)
+```
+
+The rendered output is written to a generated file (by default `deployment.rendered`), which can
+then be consumed by other Kubernetes targets such as `k8s_bundle`. The `values` field is required;
+omitting it is an error. Each target must have exactly one source file, and every placeholder in the
+template must have a corresponding key in `values`.
+
